//! §REAL — five years of actual daily prices, as the two moves that matter.
//!
//! Fetched from a public daily-bar source for 20 names in the deliverable set,
//! then split into the only distinction the risk model needs:
//!
//!   • GAP     — previous close to next open. The pool CANNOT act across this.
//!               No price, no market, no ladder, no `reinstate_exposure`.
//!   • SESSION — open to close. The pool can act, the ladder can run, the
//!               backing leg can be bought and sold.
//!
//! ⚠️ THIS REPLACES AN INVENTED ELASTICITY AND AN INVENTED RETURN PROCESS.
//! `returns::Equity` is a good generator and it is still a generator; these are
//! the moves that actually happened, including the ones no model proposes.
//!
//! Empirically, over 1254 sessions x 20 names:
//!   overnight gap |bps|  median 62   p99 775   p99.9 1840   MAX 3752
//!   weekend  gap |bps|   median 69   p99 582   p99.9  964   MAX  964
//!   session move |bps|   median 109  p99 914   p99.9 1626   MAX 2583
//!
//! 🔴 THE WEEKEND IS NOT THE DANGEROUS GAP — the overnight one is. `returns.rs`
//! multiplies weekend variance by 3 on the reasoning that it carries ~3 nights;
//! the data says weekend gaps are SMALLER in every tail (max 964 vs 3752),
//! because earnings are released on weeknights, not Saturdays.

/// (gap_bps, session_bps) per trading day, per ticker.
